WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.540
So this is a conversation where I want to
see what you think.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.270
I'm not suggesting I know what needs to happen.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:12.530
I'm not suggesting any authoritarian tactics
by states.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.570
I just want to see where you're at on this
issue.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.550
Corona virus is exploding at the national
level.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:20.990
Some states are doing well.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:26.140
But when you look at the United States as
a country in total, our worst four days in

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.050
the United States in terms of new cases came
in the last five days.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.960
So you go to the very beginning of the pandemic.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.720
The four worst days in terms of new cases
have all happened in the last week.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.730
And yet here in Massachusetts, we I'm so thrilled
to say we reported zero deaths yesterday for

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.760
the first time since the pandemic started.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:55.080
And in fact, New England and New York and
New Jersey, this sort of eight state consortium,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.120
these are some of the best places to be right
now in terms of the degree of reduction in

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.050
cases and transmission.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.720
Vermont had no new cases yesterday.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.020
New Hampshire had only 22.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.580
Maine had only 34.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.040
As I said, Massachusetts, zero deaths and
about 100 new cases.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.850
Connecticut, about 150.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.880
New York and New Jersey still with higher
case numbers, but dramatic reductions.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:22.200
And again, relative to their population, the
numbers are not quite so scary.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.190
So in terms of the consortium, our governor,
Charlie Baker, here has expanded the no quarantine

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.170
bubble to include New York, for example.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.909
Now, you're going to be able to if you travel
from New York to Massachusetts, you're not

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:40.920
going to be required to quarantine for 14
days, assuming you meet certain requirements.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:41.920
Masks are everywhere.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.760
As many of you know, I've been in Martha's
Vineyard this week.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.890
Everybody's just wearing masks.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:47.890
You go everywhere.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:48.890
It's mask, mask, mask.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.550
You're not going anywhere and seeing people
without them.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.080
But look at what's happening elsewhere.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.610
You've got these huge swaths of red states,
plus California, which are seeing record cases.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:03.900
You've got California, Texas, Florida, Arizona,
Georgia, the Carolinas, Tennessee, Louisiana,

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.900
Alabama, Ohio.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:11.709
And at some point, you're only going to keep
that walled off for so long.

00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:16.749
At some point, if the cases keep exploding
in certain parts of the country, the cases

00:02:16.749 --> 00:02:18.849
will start going up here in the northeast.

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:22.040
You you have flights between these parts of
the country.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.579
So-called mandatory quarantines are really
tough to enforce.

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:26.579
And people know it.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:27.810
People aren't doing it.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.209
So does the Northeast need to do something?

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.879
Because we we're hopefully looking at being
able to safely reopen schools in the fall

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:37.989
and do more.

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:42.140
But if the cases start bleeding over from
the more disastrous part.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.349
The country, it's going to ruin the work we've
been doing for months at this point.

00:02:46.349 --> 00:02:49.650
Again, reminder, I played this clip earlier,
I'll play it again.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:56.550
Dr. Foushee is talking about 100000 new cases
a day in the U.S. if things don't turn around.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.049
He says the infection rate is out of control
in some parts of the country.

00:03:00.049 --> 00:03:01.629
Take a look at this clip one more time.

00:03:01.629 --> 00:03:03.440
It is going to be very disturbing.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.559
I will guarantee you that, because when you
have an outbreak in one part of the country,

00:03:08.559 --> 00:03:12.040
even though in other parts of the country,
they're doing well.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.040
They are vulnerable.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.359
I made that point very clearly last week at
a press conference.

00:03:17.359 --> 00:03:21.029
We can't just focus on those areas that are
having the surge.

00:03:21.029 --> 00:03:24.150
It puts the entire country at risk.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:28.879
We are now having 40 plus thousand new cases
a day.

00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:34.939
I would not be surprised if we go up to 100000
a day if this does not turn around.

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:36.779
And so I am very concerned.

00:03:36.779 --> 00:03:38.079
So what should the Northeast do?

00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:39.639
I mean, legally, this is difficult.

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:43.049
The Constitution grants ingress and egress
to and from states.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:46.150
And I am not proposing that we violate the
Constitution.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.799
Airlines have shown they are committed to
having more flights and having flights be

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:51.829
more full.

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:54.939
So I don't know what, if anything, can be
done.

00:03:54.939 --> 00:04:01.090
But if cases continue to explode to this degree
in some parts of the country, eventually the

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.309
pandemic is going to come back to the northeast
because people are traveling.

00:04:05.309 --> 00:04:07.260
People are ignoring quarantine.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.719
Can you do roadblocks?

00:04:08.719 --> 00:04:13.300
But not to stop people from coming in merely
to register that they are coming in and ask

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.669
them, where will you be quarantining?

00:04:15.669 --> 00:04:18.049
You still can't really enforce it.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:19.259
You don't have the resources.

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:23.729
But maybe if people go through that, they
would be more mindful and maybe be more likely

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.979
to stick to the required quarantine.

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:26.979
I don't know.

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:31.379
It's obviously beyond the resources of any
state to really enforce it.

00:04:31.379 --> 00:04:32.480
So I have no idea.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.410
My question to you is, what should be done?

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.889
We are beyond the early hotspot phase.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:44.610
The early hotspot phase was based on flights
and and the earlier spots of contagion.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:45.979
You had hotspots.

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:47.389
It's now based on policy.

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:50.159
The places with the better policy are doing
better.

00:04:50.159 --> 00:04:53.879
The places with the looser policy are doing
worse.

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:54.879
What should be done?

00:04:54.879 --> 00:04:56.110
Should the Northeast do anything?

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.960
Let me know what you think and we'll have
more coverage of this on our Instagram page

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.860
at The David Pakman Show.

